'''toothy''' (tö′thi), ''a.'' [< ''tooth'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. Having teeth; full of teeth. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Let the green hops lie lightly; next expand<br>The smoothest surface with the ''toothy'' rake.
{{right|''Smart'', Hop-Garden, ii.}}}}

2. Toothsome. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|A certain relaxation subsequently occurs, during which meat or game which is at first tough becomes more tender and ''toothy''. ''Alien. and Neurol.'', X. 459.}}

3. Biting; carping; crabbed; peevish. [Prov. Eng. and Scotch.]

{{smaller block|''Toothy'' critics by the score,<br>In bloody raw [row]. ''Burns'', To W. Creech.}}

'''tooting-hill†''' (tö′ting-hil), ''n.'' [< ME. ''totynghylle, tytynge-hylle''; < ''tooting'', verbal n. of ''toot''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', + ''hill''<sup>1</sup>.] Same as ''toothill''. ''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 497.

'''tooting-hole†''' (tö′ting-hōl), ''n.'' [< ME. ''totynghole''; < ''tooting'', verbal n. of ''toot''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', + ''hole''<sup>1</sup>.] A spy-hole.

{{smaller block|They within the citee perceived well this ''totyng-hole'', and laied a pece of ordynaunce directly against the wyndowe. ''Hall'', Hen. VI., an. 6.}}

'''tooting-place†''' (tö′ting-plās), ''n.'' [ME. ''totyngplace''; < ''tooting'', verbal n. of ''toot''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', + ''place''.] A watch-tower.

{{smaller block|''Toting place.'' ''Wyclif'', Isa. xxi. 5.}}

'''tootle''' (tö′tl), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''tootled'', ppr. ''tootling''. [Freq. or dim. of ''toot''<sup>2</sup>.] To toot gently or repeatedly; especially, to produce a succession of weak modulated sounds upon a flute.

readings.c3s1:
Hence, from Teut., OF. ''tope'', dim. ''toupet'', F. ''toupet'', tuft of hair, crest, top, knob, = Sp. ''tope'' = It. ''toppo'', end. Cf. ''tip''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A tuft or crest on the apex or summit of anything, as a helmet, the head, etc.; hence, the hair of the head; especially, the forelock.

{{smaller block|His ''top'' was dokked lyk a preest beforn.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 590.}}
Let 's take the instant by the forward ''top''.
{{right|''Shak.'', All's Well, v. 3. 39.}}}}

'''2.''' Any bunch of hair, fibers, or filaments; specifically, in ''woolen-manuf.'', a bundle of long-staple combed wool-slivers, ready for the spinner, and weighing 1½ pounds.

{{smaller block|A ''toppe'' of flax, de lin le toup.
{{right|''Rel. Antiq.'' (ed. Halliwell and Wright), II. 78.}}
This long fibre, . . . which is called the ''top'' in the worsted manufacture.
{{right|''W. C. Bramwell'', Wool-Carding, p. 27.}}}}

'''3.''' The crown of the head, or the upper surface of the head back of the forehead; the vertex or sinciput.

{{smaller block|Thou take hym by the ''toppe'' and I by the tayle;
A sorowfull songe in faith he shall singe.
{{right|''Chester Plays'', ii. 176. (''Halliwell.'')}}
All the starred vengeance of heaven fall
On her ingrateful ''top!''
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, ii. 4. 165.}}}}

'''4.''' The highest or uppermost part of anything; the most elevated end or point; the summit; the apex.

{{smaller block|Pieres the Plowman al the place me shewed,
And bad me toten on the tree on ''toppe'' and on rote.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xvi. 22.}}
Their statues are very fairely erected in Alabaster vppon the ''toppe'' of the monument.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 52.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|And long the way appears, . . .
And high the mountain-''tops'', in cloudy air,
The mountain-''tops'' where is the throne of Truth.
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Thyrsis.}}}}

Specifically — (''a'') The head or upper part of a plant, especially the above-ground part of plants yielding root-crops: as, potato-''tops'', turnip-''tops''; in ''phar.'', the newer growing parts of a plant.

{{smaller block|If the buds are made our food, they are called heads, or ''tops''; so cabbage heads, heads of asparagus and artichoke.
{{right|''Watts'', Logic, I. vi. § 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|The fruits and ''tops'' of juniper are the only officinal parts.
{{right|''U. S. Dispensatory'', 14th ed., p. 827.}}}}

(''b'') The upper part of a shoe. Compare def. 13 and ''top-boot.''

{{smaller block|He has ''tops'' to his shoes up to his mid leg.
{{right|''Farquhar'', Beaux' Stratagem, iii. 1.}}}}

(''c'') The upper end or source; head waters, as of a river. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The third navigable river is called Toppahanock. . . . At the ''top'' of it inhabit the people called Mannahoacks amongst the mountaines.
{{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, I. 117.}}}}

(''d'') The upper side; the surface.

{{smaller block|Such trees as spread their roots near the ''top'' of the ground.
{{right|''Bacon.''}}}}

(''e'') ''pl.'' The collection of a few copies of each sheet of a printed book placed on the top of a pile of such printed sheets.

5. That which is first or foremost. (''a'') The beginning: noting time. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|In thende of Octob'r, or in the ''toppe''
Of Novemb'r in the lande is hem to stoppe.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 96.}}}}

(''b'') That which comes first in the natural or the accepted order; the first or upper part; the head: as, the ''top'' of a page; the ''top'' of a column of figures.

{{smaller block|''Cade.'' What is thy name?
''Clerk.'' Emmanuel.}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|''Dick.'' They use to write it on the ''top'' of letters.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iv. 2. 107.}}}}

{{smaller block|Ralph left her at the ''top'' of Regent Street, and turned down a by-thoroughfare. ''Dickens'', Nicholas Nickleby, x.}}

{{smaller|(''c'') The most advanced or prominent part; the highest part, place, rank, grade, or the like.}}

{{smaller block|Take a boy from the ''top'' of a grammar school, and one of the same age, bred . . . in his father's family, . . . and . . . see which of the two will have the more manly carriage. ''Locke'', Education, § 70.}}

{{smaller block|Home was head; his brilliant composition and thorough knowledge of the books brought him to the ''top''.
{{right|''Farrar'', Julian Home, xix.}}}}

6. The crowning-point. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|He was upon the ''top'' of his marriage with Magdaleine the French King's daughter.
{{right|''Knolles'', Hist. of the Turks. (''Latham.'')}}}}

7. The highest point or degree; pinnacle; zenith; climax.

{{smaller block|What valiant foemen, like to autumn's corn,
Have we mow'd down in ''tops'' of all their pride!
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen VI., v. 7. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|Thus by that Noise without, and this within,
She summon'd was unto the ''top'' of fear.
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, iv. 215.}}}}

{{smaller block|And when my hope was at the ''top'', I still was worst mistaken, O. ''Burns'', My Father was a Farmer.}}

8. The highest example, type, or representative; chief; crown; consummation; acme.

{{smaller block|Godliness being the chiefest ''top'' and well-spring of all true virtues, even as God is of all good things.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, v. 1.}}}}
